    if (", "correct_answer": "(signed char)b < -128"}, {"hint": "Recall that hex color is #RRGGBB and convert 80 from hex to decimal.", "type": "quiz", "correct": 0, "heading": "Knowledge Check", "options": ["128", "255", "0", "16"], "question": "You're given a hex color #0080FF. What is the decimal value of the green component?"}]

[{"body": "Imagine you're at a restaurant. You order food, and the chef prepares it. While you wait, the hostess manages other customers. In computing, a process is like your food order. It's a program in execution, managed by the OS. Just as the chef and hostess work together to keep things running smoothly, the OS juggles processes, handles, and threads to keep your computer humming.", "type": "concept", "heading": "Programs in Motion"}, {"body": "When a program runs, the OS creates a process for it. A process has its own memory space, and the OS assigns a unique ID, called a handle. Think of a handle like a restaurant order number. The OS also manages threads, which are like mini-tasks within a process. Just as a chef might prep multiple dishes at once, a process can have multiple threads executing concurrently.", "type": "concept", "heading": "Under the Hood"}, {"body": "This C example creates a new process using fork().", "code": "#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    pid_t pid;
    // Create a new process
    pid = fork();
    if (pid < 0) {
        // Error occurred
        perror("fork");
        exit(1);
    } else if (pid == 0) {
        // Child process
        printf("Hello from child!\n");
    } else {
        // Parent process
        printf("Hello from parent!\n");
    }
    return 0;
}", "type": "code", "heading": "Basic Process Example"}, {"type": "code_practice", "context": "Use the perror function to print an error message for "fork".", "heading": "Fill in the Error Message", "code_after": "");
